  "preamble": "Implosivization of a voiceless coronal stop: plain [t] acquires glottal closure and voicing and becomes the implosive (or preglottalized) [ɗ]. It runs parallel to p > ɓ in the same languages. In Vietnamese, after voiced *d had devoiced and merged with *t (leaving a low-register tone), t > ɗ re-introduced an implosive (Ferlus 1992), so Proto-Vietic *t and *d surface as đ [ʔɗ]; the gap left by t was refilled by s > t. Khmer has the same outcome: Old Khmer words transliterated with t (and Proto-Mon-Khmer *t) are pronounced with [ɗ] today.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Implosivization, Glottalization, Voicing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Vietic to Vietnamese",
      "languageFamily": "Vietic",
